(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-venous-disorders-quiz"]={"title":"Venous Disorders Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which mimic of deep vein thrombosis presents with leg pain, erythema, fever and chills?","choices":["Lymphedema","Cellulitis","Ruptured popliteal cyst","Calf muscle tear"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis causes leg pain with erythema and may be accompanied by fever and chills. Deep vein thrombosis (DVT) can also bring pain, warmth and erythema, so the fever and chills are the clues that tip this mimic toward infection. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which form of immobility counts as an item in the Wells criteria?","choices":["A long-haul airline flight","A car trip of several hours","A desk job with long sitting","Bedridden for more than 3 days"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Wells item reads bedridden recently for more than 3 days or major surgery within 12 weeks, worth 1 point. Paralysis, paresis or recent plaster immobilization of the leg is a separate item; ordinary travel or sitting is not scored. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which examination findings characterize a healthy hemodialysis fistula?","choices":["Faint thrill with no bruit","Tender cord with overlying warmth","Cool skin and absent pulses","Diffuse thrill and soft bruit"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A healthy hemodialysis arteriovenous (AV) fistula has a diffuse thrill and a soft bruit, both with systolic and diastolic components, and it collapses completely when the arm is elevated. Upper arm or forearm sites are generally preferred for this access. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"Which surgery for chronic venous insufficiency is occasionally successful yet has a high rate of postoperative deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Valvuloplasty","Venous bypass","Vein stripping","Injection sclerotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Valvuloplasty is occasionally successful, but the incidence of postoperative deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is high. Surgery for chronic venous insufficiency aims to occlude or remove the affected vessels, restore proper flow and decrease venous hypertension. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"In the high likelihood Wells category, what does a negative D-dimer change?","choices":["It calls for venography","It allows anticoagulation to wait","Nothing; ultrasound is still done","It rules out the clot"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In the high-likelihood category the D-dimer is optional, and whether it is positive or negative, the patient goes to ultrasound of the thigh. The D-dimer's rule-out role applies only to low-likelihood patients. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How is a congenital arteriovenous fistula usually managed?","choices":["Conservatively, with elastic support hose","Excision of its single communication","Lifelong anticoagulation","Thrombolysis with a filter"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Congenital arteriovenous (AV) fistulas are difficult to treat because the communications can be numerous, so they are treated conservatively with elastic support hose. Embolization can sometimes remove the fistula, whereas acquired fistulas are managed surgically. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Which drug class commonly causes bilateral leg swelling without signs of inflammation?","choices":["Loop diuretics","Calcium channel blockers","Low molecular weight heparins","Direct factor Xa inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Drug-induced edema is a common adverse effect of some drugs, including dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers such as amlodipine. The swelling is often bilateral and usually shows no signs of inflammation, which separates it from deep vein thrombosis (DVT). — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Without treatment, what is the cumulative rate of fatal pulmonary embolism when the embolism is symptomatic and massive?","choices":["Under 1%","About 3%","About 9%","About 30%"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Without adequate treatment, the cumulative rate of fatal pulmonary embolism is about 9% in symptomatic massive pulmonary embolism. That compares with 3% for symptomatic nonmassive embolism and under 1% for symptomatic deep vein thrombosis (DVT) without embolism. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Prior deep vein thrombosis is one of the most common causes of which chronic condition?","choices":["Superficial thrombophlebitis","Arteriovenous fistula","Peripheral artery disease","Chronic venous insufficiency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prior deep vein thrombosis (DVT) is one of the most common causes of chronic venous insufficiency, even though about 25% of patients have no known history of DVT. Secondary chronic venous insufficiency comes from obstruction or valvular incompetence after a DVT, with the valve leaflets thickened and scarred. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the gold standard test to confirm superficial thrombophlebitis?","choices":["Computed tomography angiography","Venous duplex ultrasound","D-dimer measurement","Contrast ascending venography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Superficial thrombophlebitis is diagnosed from history and physical examination and confirmed by venous duplex ultrasound, the gold standard. It shows a noncompressible superficial vein with clot and a thickened wall; thickening without clot would be phlebitis. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which are the most commonly affected proximal veins in lower extremity deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Saphenous and perforator","Femoral and popliteal","Peroneal and tibial","Iliac and caval"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In lower extremity deep vein thrombosis (DVT), the most commonly affected proximal veins are the femoral and popliteal veins in the thigh. The most common distal veins are the peroneal veins in the calves, and in pregnancy the pelvic veins are involved. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which finding is most sensitive for a lower extremity deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Leg warmth","A positive Homan sign","Leg swelling","Leg pain"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leg swelling is 97% sensitive for lower extremity deep vein thrombosis (DVT), ahead of pain (86%) and warmth (72%). Erythema and a persisting calf cramp can also occur, while a positive Homan sign is neither sensitive nor specific. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Anticoagulation","Thrombolysis","Inferior vena cava filter","Thrombectomy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation is the mainstay of treatment for deep vein thrombosis (DVT). Depending on the situation, patients may also need thrombolysis or lifelong anticoagulation, and three strategies deliver it: heparin bridged to warfarin, parenteral therapy then dabigatran or edoxaban, or oral rivaroxaban or apixaban. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"In the low likelihood Wells category, a patient's D-dimer is positive and the thigh ultrasound that follows shows no clot. What is the next step?","choices":["End the work-up there","Rescan the thigh in a week","Contrast venography","Start anticoagulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the low-likelihood pathway a positive D-dimer leads to ultrasound of the thigh, and a negative ultrasound ends the work-up. Further imaging after a negative thigh ultrasound, such as a lower leg scan, phlebography or a repeat scan in 1 week, belongs to the high-likelihood pathway. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 56, 57 (flowchart picture)"},{"q":"Erythema extending well beyond the margin of an inflamed vein raises suspicion of which condition?","choices":["Lipodermatosclerosis","Septic thrombophlebitis","Superficial phlebitis","Stasis dermatitis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Erythema that extends significantly beyond the margin of the vein should raise suspicion of septic thrombophlebitis, an infection within the vein. High fever, fluctuance and purulent drainage point the same way, and septic thrombophlebitis is uncommon without a history of venous cannulation. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"Which oral agent may help accelerate healing of venous ulcers?","choices":["Furosemide","Warfarin","Aspirin","Prednisone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin may help to accelerate healing of venous ulcers, even though no oral medication has yet been proven useful for the treatment of venous disease. Flavonoids, used to increase venous tone, are the other oral agent mentioned. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slides 34-35, 66"},{"q":"How long is anticoagulation given after a venous thromboembolism provoked by a minor transient risk factor?","choices":["9 to 12 months","An indefinite period","3 to 6 months","Less than 3 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A venous thromboembolism provoked by a minor transient risk factor, such as estrogen therapy, pregnancy, minor surgery, prolonged travel or minor leg injury, is treated for 3 to 6 months. A major transient factor gets 3 months and an unprovoked clot is treated indefinitely. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Why can a positive D-dimer not confirm deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Many conditions raise it","It is often falsely negative","It detects only arterial clots","It shows the clot location"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An elevated D-dimer implies increased fibrinolysis, and the test is sensitive but not specific, so conditions other than deep vein thrombosis (DVT) can raise it. A positive result therefore leads to venous duplex ultrasound to confirm a clot. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Older adults with a lower extremity wound need which measurement alongside the arterial pulse examination?","choices":["Skin punch biopsy","Contrast venography","Ankle-brachial index","D-dimer level"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Arterial pulse examination combined with the ankle-brachial index (ABI) should be performed on any patient with a lower extremity wound, and in older patients. History and examination alone cannot reliably exclude other causes of a leg ulcer, so the arterial side is checked directly. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Which chronic complication follows deep vein thrombosis 6 to 24 months later?","choices":["Post-thrombotic syndrome","Lymphedema","Arteriovenous fistula","Superficial thrombophlebitis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Post-thrombotic (postphlebitic) syndrome is symptomatic chronic venous insufficiency that follows a deep vein thrombosis (DVT), appearing from 6 to 24 months afterward. It sits on the differential of a new DVT because it also causes a swollen, symptomatic leg. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which added factors raise the deep vein thrombosis risk of oral contraceptive pill use?","choices":["Regular exercise","Low body weight","A vegetarian diet","Tobacco use or obesity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral contraceptive pills raise deep vein thrombosis (DVT) risk as an acquired hypercoagulable state, especially if the patient also smokes tobacco or is obese. Both smoking and being overweight or obese are also listed as DVT risk factors on their own. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How long is conservative stocking therapy generally tried before ablation is offered for varicose veins?","choices":["One to two years","Weeks to months","A single visit","Several days"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A trial of conservative therapy with stockings is generally done for weeks to months before venous ablation therapy is offered. Conservative care for isolated varicose veins means leg elevation, exercise and compression, once arterial disease has been ruled out. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What underlies the wide spectrum of abnormalities in chronic venous disease?","choices":["Chronic arterial hypoperfusion","Lymphatic vessel obstruction","Loss of plasma protein into tissue","Venous hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Venous hypertension, elevated venous pressure, underlies many of the wide spectrum of abnormalities of chronic venous disease, from telangiectasias to varicose veins to chronic venous insufficiency. It triggers a sequence of venous dilation, then skin changes and ulceration. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"What is first-line management of uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis?","choices":["Inferior vena cava filter","Anticoagulation for three months","Warm compresses and ibuprofen","Thrombolysis with alteplase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Uncomplicated superficial thrombophlebitis rarely causes serious complications, and typical treatment is warm compresses, compression, elevation of the limb and a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) such as ibuprofen or diclofenac. Anticoagulation is added only for extensive clot burden or intermediate or higher thrombotic risk. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"Intermittent pneumatic compression prevents clots chiefly by countering which component of the Virchow triad?","choices":["Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma","All three components"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Intermittent pneumatic compression squeezes the leg in sequence from the lower leg upward, pushing blood along and countering venous stasis. It is used especially when a patient must be immobilized after surgery or during a long hospital stay. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Andexanet alfa is the reversal agent for which pair of anticoagulants?","choices":["Fondaparinux and dabigatran","Apixaban and rivaroxaban","Dabigatran and warfarin","Heparin and enoxaparin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Andexanet alfa (Andexxa) is the reversal agent for apixaban and rivaroxaban when either causes life-threatening or intracranial hemorrhage, the most serious adverse effect of anticoagulation. It is also used off-label for edoxaban. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Prolonged bedridden inactivity raises superficial thrombophlebitis risk. Which Virchow triad component does immobilization represent?","choices":["Arterial insufficiency","Venous stasis","Hypercoagulable state","Endothelial trauma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Immobilization, such as long flights, prolonged sitting or postoperative inactivity, is listed under venous stasis, the altered blood flow component of the Virchow triad. Being bedridden alters blood flow in the leg veins rather than injuring them or changing the blood's clotting tendency. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which is a risk factor for chronic venous disease?","choices":["Ligamentous laxity (flat feet)","Male sex","Lower body mass index","Regular calf muscle exercise"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ligamentous laxity, such as flat feet, is a listed risk factor for chronic venous disease, along with advancing age, female sex, family history, increased body mass index, smoking, lower extremity trauma, prior venous thrombosis, higher parity and high estrogen states. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Which three components make up the Virchow triad?","choices":["Stasis, hypertension, endothelial trauma","Stasis, infection, vessel spasm","Immobility, anemia, dehydration","Stasis, hypercoagulability, endothelial trauma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The Virchow triad groups deep vein thrombosis (DVT) risk factors into venous stasis (altered blood flow), hypercoagulable states (inherited or acquired) and vascular endothelial injury (trauma). Immobilization, heart failure, cancer, pregnancy and surgery each fit one of these three. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Which study is the gold standard for diagnosing deep vein thrombosis?","choices":["Plasma D-dimer","Magnetic resonance venography","Contrast ascending venography","Venous duplex ultrasound"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Contrast ascending venography is the definitive gold standard for diagnosing deep vein thrombosis (DVT). In practice it has been replaced by venous duplex ultrasound of the involved extremity, which is why the gold standard and the usual first imaging test differ. — CMS I - Venous Disorders - Shah Fallsv UPDATED.pptx, Slide 57"}]};
